JAVA COLLECTIONS — INTERVIEW ANSWERS 🔴
Questions 101–135
101. What is Collection Framework?

Answer:

The Java Collection Framework is a set of interfaces, classes, and utility methods used to store and manipulate groups of objects.

Main interfaces are:

List
Set
Queue
Map — technically part of the Collections Framework, but it does not extend Collection.

Example:

List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

names.add("John");
names.add("David");
names.add("John");

System.out.println(names);

Output:

[John, David, John]

Interview point: Collections are useful in automation when handling test data, web elements, API responses, configuration data, etc.

102. Collection vs Collections?

This is a very common interview question.

Collection

Collection is an interface.

It is the root interface for interfaces such as:

Collection
   |
   +-- List
   +-- Set
   +-- Queue

Example:

Collection<String> data = new ArrayList<>();
Collections

Collections is a utility class containing static methods.

Examples:

Collections.sort(list);
Collections.reverse(list);
Collections.shuffle(list);
Collections.max(list);
Collections.min(list);
Short answer:

Collection is an interface, whereas Collections is a utility class containing static methods for operating on collections.

103. List vs Set vs Map?
Feature	List	Set	Map
Stores	Values	Values	Key-value pairs
Duplicates	Allowed	Not allowed	Keys not allowed; values allowed
Order	Usually maintains insertion order	Depends on implementation	Depends on implementation
Index	Yes	No	No
Example	ArrayList	HashSet	HashMap

Example:

List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();

Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();

Map<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();
Interview answer:

I use List when duplicates and ordering are required, Set when uniqueness is required, and Map when I need key-value based lookup.

104. What is ArrayList?

ArrayList is a resizable array implementation of List.

ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

names.add("John");
names.add("David");
names.add("Alex");

System.out.println(names.get(1));

Output:

David
Features
Allows duplicates
Maintains insertion order
Allows null
Fast random access using index
Not synchronized
Internally uses a dynamically growing array
When would you use it?

I use ArrayList when I frequently access elements by index and don't have frequent insertions/deletions in the middle.

105. What is LinkedList?

LinkedList implements both:

List
Deque

It is based on a doubly linked list.

LinkedList<String> list = new LinkedList<>();

list.add("A");
list.add("B");
list.addFirst("Start");
list.addLast("End");

System.out.println(list);

Output:

[Start, A, B, End]
Advantages

Insertion/removal at known positions can be efficient.

Disadvantage

Random access is slower than ArrayList.

list.get(100);

requires traversing nodes.

106. What is Vector?

Vector is a legacy synchronized List implementation.

Vector<String> vector = new Vector<>();

vector.add("Java");
vector.add("Selenium");

Important points:

Maintains insertion order
Allows duplicates
Allows null
Synchronized
Legacy class
Generally slower than ArrayList because of synchronization
Interview answer:

Vector is a legacy synchronized collection. In modern applications, I generally prefer ArrayList unless synchronization is specifically required.

107. What is Stack?

Stack is a legacy class that follows LIFO:

Last In, First Out.

Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();

stack.push(10);
stack.push(20);
stack.push(30);

System.out.println(stack.pop());

Output:

30

Common methods:

push()
pop()
peek()
empty()
Modern alternative

Deque / ArrayDeque is generally preferred for stack behavior.

108. What is HashSet?

HashSet stores unique elements.

Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();

set.add("Java");
set.add("Selenium");
set.add("Java");

System.out.println(set);

The duplicate "Java" is not stored.

Features
No duplicates
Allows one null
No guaranteed insertion order
Uses hashing internally
Fast average add, remove, and contains
109. What is LinkedHashSet?

LinkedHashSet is a HashSet that maintains insertion order.

Set<String> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();

set.add("C");
set.add("A");
set.add("B");

System.out.println(set);

Output:

[C, A, B]
Interview answer:

If I need unique elements and also need to preserve insertion order, I use LinkedHashSet.

110. What is TreeSet?

TreeSet stores unique elements in sorted order.

Set<Integer> numbers = new TreeSet<>();

numbers.add(50);
numbers.add(10);
numbers.add(30);
numbers.add(10);

System.out.println(numbers);

Output:

[10, 30, 50]
Features
No duplicates
Sorted order
Generally implemented using a Red-Black tree
Operations are typically O(log n)
Does not allow null in the usual natural-order case
111. What is HashMap?

HashMap stores data in key-value pairs.

Map<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();

map.put(101, "John");
map.put(102, "David");

System.out.println(map.get(101));

Output:

John
Features
Keys must be unique
Values can be duplicated
Allows one null key
Allows multiple null values
No guaranteed iteration order
Not synchronized

Example:

map.put(101, "John");
map.put(101, "Alex");

The second value replaces the first.

112. What is LinkedHashMap?

LinkedHashMap maintains insertion order of entries.

Map<Integer, String> map = new LinkedHashMap<>();

map.put(3, "C");
map.put(1, "A");
map.put(2, "B");

System.out.println(map);

Output:

{3=C, 1=A, 2=B}
Use case

When I need HashMap-like key-value lookup but also need predictable insertion order, I use LinkedHashMap.

113. What is TreeMap?

TreeMap stores key-value pairs sorted by keys.

Map<Integer, String> map = new TreeMap<>();

map.put(30, "C");
map.put(10, "A");
map.put(20, "B");

System.out.println(map);

Output:

{10=A, 20=B, 30=C}
Important

TreeMap sorts keys, not values.

Typically:

O(log n)

for basic operations.

114. What is Hashtable?

Hashtable is a legacy synchronized Map implementation.

Hashtable<Integer, String> table = new Hashtable<>();

table.put(1, "Java");
table.put(2, "Selenium");

Important differences from HashMap:

Synchronized
Does not allow null key
Does not allow null values
Legacy
Generally slower than HashMap in non-concurrent use
115. ArrayList vs LinkedList?
ArrayList	LinkedList
Dynamic array	Doubly linked list
Fast random access	Slower random access
get(index) fast	get(index) slower
Insert/delete in middle can require shifting	Node insertion/removal can be efficient when position/node is known
Usually preferred	Useful for deque/list operations
Interview answer:

If my application frequently reads elements by index, I prefer ArrayList. If I frequently perform insertions/removals and need linked-list/deque behavior, LinkedList can be appropriate.

116. HashSet vs TreeSet?
HashSet	TreeSet
No duplicates	No duplicates
No guaranteed ordering	Sorted order
Hash-based	Tree-based
Average O(1) basic operations	O(log n)
Faster generally	Slower but sorted
Example:
HashSet<Integer> h = new HashSet<>();
TreeSet<Integer> t = new TreeSet<>();

Use HashSet for uniqueness.

Use TreeSet when uniqueness + sorting is required.

117. HashMap vs Hashtable?
HashMap	Hashtable
Not synchronized	Synchronized
Allows one null key	Does not allow null key
Allows null values	Does not allow null values
Modern	Legacy
Generally faster	Generally slower
Interview answer:

I normally prefer HashMap. If thread-safe map operations are required, I would consider modern concurrent collections such as ConcurrentHashMap rather than choosing Hashtable for new code.

118. HashMap vs TreeMap?
HashMap	TreeMap
No guaranteed order	Sorted by keys
Hash-based	Tree-based
Average O(1) basic operations	O(log n)
Allows null key	Null key generally not allowed with natural ordering
Faster for lookup generally	Useful when sorted keys are required
119. List vs Set?
List

Allows duplicates.

List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

list.add(10);
list.add(10);

Result:

[10, 10]
Set

Does not allow duplicates.

Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();

set.add(10);
set.add(10);

Result:

[10]
Interview answer:

I use List when duplicate values or index-based access are required. I use Set when uniqueness is important.

120. How does HashMap work internally? 🔴 VERY IMPORTANT

This is a high-priority SDET interview question.

When you execute:

map.put("Java", 100);

HashMap roughly performs these steps:

Step 1 — Calculate hash

HashMap obtains the key's hash value.

"Java" → hash
Step 2 — Determine bucket

The hash is used to determine the bucket/index where the entry should be stored.

Step 3 — Check the bucket

If the bucket is empty:

store entry

If entries already exist:

compare hash
        ↓
compare keys using equals()
Step 4 — Collision handling

If different keys map to the same bucket, HashMap handles the collision using its bucket structure.

In modern Java, heavily populated buckets can be treeified under the required conditions, improving worst-case lookup behavior compared with a long linked chain.

Step 5 — Retrieve

For:

map.get("Java");

HashMap calculates the hash again, locates the bucket, and checks the candidate entries using hash/key comparisons.

Strong interview answer:

HashMap stores key-value pairs using hashing. During put, it calculates the key's hash, determines a bucket, and stores the entry there. During get, it calculates the hash again and searches the corresponding bucket. If multiple keys map to the same bucket, collision handling is performed using the bucket's internal structure. equals() is used to identify the exact key.

121. What happens when two keys have the same hash?

Suppose:

key1.hashCode() == key2.hashCode()

This does not necessarily mean the keys are equal.

They can go to the same bucket.

HashMap then uses equality comparison to determine whether they represent the same key.

Conceptually:

Same hash
   ↓
Same bucket
   ↓
equals()
   ↓
Same key? → replace value
Different key? → store another entry
122. What is collision?

A collision occurs when two different keys produce the same bucket/index during hashing.

Example conceptually:

Key A → bucket 5
Key B → bucket 5

Both need to be stored in the same bucket.

HashMap handles this through its internal bucket structure.

Interview answer:

A hash collision occurs when different keys map to the same bucket. HashMap handles the collision internally and uses key equality to distinguish the entries.

123. What is load factor?

Load factor determines when a HashMap should resize its internal table as it grows.

Default load factor is:

0.75

For example, conceptually:

capacity = 16
load factor = 0.75

threshold = 16 × 0.75
          = 12

When the map grows beyond its threshold, it resizes.

Interview answer:

Load factor controls how full a HashMap can become before resizing. The default load factor is 0.75.

124. Default HashMap capacity?

The commonly cited default initial capacity is:

16

and default load factor is:

0.75

Important interview nuance: HashMap uses lazy initialization in modern Java, so the internal table is not necessarily allocated at the exact moment you execute:

new HashMap<>();

The table is initialized when needed.

125. Can HashMap contain null?

Yes.

HashMap allows:

one null key
multiple null values

Example:

Map<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();

map.put(null, "Java");
map.put(1, null);
map.put(2, null);

Valid.

126. Can HashSet contain duplicates?

No.

Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();

set.add("Java");
set.add("Java");

Only one "Java" is stored.

127. How does HashSet identify duplicates?

This is another very important question.

HashSet internally uses a HashMap.

Conceptually:

HashSet element
      ↓
HashMap key
      ↓
hashCode()
      ↓
equals()

When adding an object:

set.add(object);

Java uses:

hashCode()
equals()

to determine whether an equivalent element already exists.

Important interview point

If you create your own class and override equals(), you should generally override hashCode() consistently.

class Employee {

    int id;

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Integer.hashCode(id);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        // equality logic
        return true;
    }
}
Interview answer:

HashSet uses hashCode() to locate the appropriate bucket and equals() to determine whether an equivalent element already exists.

128. What is Iterator?

Iterator is used to traverse collection elements one by one.

List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();

list.add("Java");
list.add("Selenium");
list.add("API");

Iterator<String> iterator = list.iterator();

while (iterator.hasNext()) {
    System.out.println(iterator.next());
}

Methods:

hasNext()
next()
remove()
Important

Iterator provides a standard way to traverse collections.

129. Iterator vs ListIterator?
Iterator	ListIterator
Works with Collection	Specifically for List
Forward traversal	Forward + backward
hasNext()	hasNext()
next()	next()
No previous()	Has previous()
Limited modification	Can add/set/remove elements

Example:

ListIterator<String> it = list.listIterator();

while (it.hasNext()) {
    System.out.println(it.next());
}

while (it.hasPrevious()) {
    System.out.println(it.previous());
}
130. Comparable vs Comparator? 🔴
Comparable

Used for natural/default ordering.

Class implements:

Comparable<T>

and overrides:

compareTo()

Example:

class Employee implements Comparable<Employee> {

    int salary;

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Employee e) {
        return this.salary - e.salary;
    }
}
Comparator

Used when you want custom/external sorting logic.

Comparator<Employee> bySalary =
    (e1, e2) -> Integer.compare(e1.salary, e2.salary);
Difference
Comparable	Comparator
compareTo()	compare()
Defined inside class	Separate comparison logic
Natural ordering	Custom ordering
Usually one primary natural ordering	Can create multiple sorting strategies
Interview answer:

Comparable is used when the class itself defines its natural ordering. Comparator is used when I want separate or multiple custom sorting strategies.

131. How do you sort ArrayList?
Method 1 — Collections.sort()
List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();

numbers.add(30);
numbers.add(10);
