Don't stop at definitions. A 2nd-round interviewer can turn these into scenarios:

Q1. You receive 500 employee IDs from an API. How do you check duplicates?

Answer: Use a HashSet.

Set<String> uniqueIds = new HashSet<>();

for (String id : ids) {
    if (!uniqueIds.add(id)) {
        System.out.println("Duplicate: " + id);
    }
}

Q2. You need to maintain employee ID → employee name. Which collection?

Map<Integer, String> employees = new HashMap<>();

Q3. You need unique values but want original insertion order.

LinkedHashSet

Q4. You need unique values in sorted order.

TreeSet

Q5. You need key-value pairs sorted by key.

TreeMap

Q6. You need key-value pairs while preserving insertion order.

LinkedHashMap

Q7. You need to count occurrences of API response values.

Map<String, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();

Q8. You have a List of web-element texts and want unique values.

Set<String> uniqueTexts =
        new LinkedHashSet<>(texts);

Q9. You need to sort employee objects by salary.

Use:

Comparator<Employee>

Q10. Why would you choose HashMap instead of ArrayList?

When I need key-based lookup rather than index-based access.

⭐ One line to remember for interviews
LIST → duplicates + index
SET  → unique values
MAP  → key + value

HashSet      → unique, no guaranteed order
LinkedHashSet → unique + insertion order
TreeSet      → unique + sorted

HashMap       → key/value, no guaranteed order
LinkedHashMap → key/value + insertion order
TreeMap       → key/value + sorted keys

These 35 questions are enough to cover the core Collections portion, but for a real SDET 2nd/client round, the most important next step is to practice the Collections coding problems—especially frequency counting, duplicates, sorting objects, converting collections, HashMap problems, and ArrayList/Set scenarios.
